  "output": "This confirms it — no hooks, no state, no data fetching on the client anywhere yet. Every `\"use client\"` component is a static `motion` entrance animation with no `useEffect`/subscriptions to leak. Here's the honest analysis.\n\n## Reality check first\n\nThis codebase is at Phase 0–1 (per `HANDOFF.md`): only the landing page, get-started, and sources-empty-state screens are wired. The entire `engine/` pipeline — `normalize.py`, `blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `graph.py`, `db.py`, `main.py` — is `raise NotImplementedError(...)` stubs. There is **no loop-based query code, no client-side data fetching, no matching/resolution logic to profile yet.** So most of \"find N+1 patterns / re-renders / leaks\" has nothing concrete to point at in engine/ — I'm not going to invent bugs in code that doesn't exist.\n\nWhat I can give you: (1) real, actionable issues in the ~10 files that do exist, and (2) specific guardrails to build into Phase 2/3 *before* the classic version of each bug gets written, since the shapes that cause them (per-conflict resolution loop, entity-pair scoring loop, batch writes) are already implied by the schema and AGENTS.md.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Real issues today (web layer)\n\n**Duplicate query risk — `web/lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`**\n`countSourcesBySide()` calls `listSources()`, which re-runs the full `select().from(sources)`. If a future console header renders both the table (`listSources`) and a stat strip (`countSourcesBySide`) on the same page, that's two round trips for identical data in one request.\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n`React.cache` dedupes by-argument within a single request/render pass — free, no extra dependency, and it's the correct tool since AGENTS.md keeps this Server-Component-only.\n\nAlso minor: `countSourcesBySide` does three separate `.filter()` passes over the same array — fine at current scale (sources is a handful of rows, not entities), but a single pass is just as easy:\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return all.reduce(\n    (acc, s) => {\n      if (s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\") acc.crm++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\") acc.warehouse++;\n      else acc.synthetic++;\n      return acc;\n    },\n    { crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 },\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Postgres pool has no bounds — `web/lib/db/index.ts:38`**\n```ts\nconst sql = globalForDb.canonSql ?? postgres(url, { prepare: false });\n```\nNo `max`/`idle_timeout`. Fine locally; if this ever deploys to a serverless/edge runtime (Vercel functions), each cold instance opens its own pool with the driver's default `max: 10`, and Supabase's pooler has a hard connection ceiling — a burst of concurrent function invocations can exhaust it. Since you're already using Supabase's transaction pooler (`prepare: false` confirms this), cap it low:\n```ts\nconst sql = globalForDb.canonSql ?? postgres(url, { prepare: false, max: 1, idle_timeout: 20 });\n```\nThe globalThis caching pattern here is otherwise correct — good HMR hygiene, keep it.\n\n**React re-renders / leaks:** nothing to flag. `nav-rail.tsx` hoists its `NAV` array outside the component (no re-creation per render) and its only state is `usePathname()`, which is supposed to re-render on navigation. No `useEffect`, no manual subscriptions, no `setInterval` exist in the tree yet — so there's no leak to find.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Where these bugs *will* get written (Phase 2/3) — build it right the first time\n\n**N+1 in `db.py` writes.** The docstring says \"a run produces thousands of rows across `entities`, `matches`, `conflicts`\" — if `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` get implemented as a per-row `INSERT` in a loop, that's thousands of round trips per run. Use psycopg3's batch execution:\n```python\n# engine/db.py\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    with get_conn() as conn, conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.executemany(\n            \"\"\"INSERT INTO entities (run_id, source_id, external_id, entity_type, fields,\n                                       parent_external_id, last_modified_at)\n               VALUES (%s, %s, %s, %s, %s, %s, %s)\"\"\",\n            [\n                (run_id, e.source_id, e.external_id, e.entity_type, e.model_dump_json(),\n                 e.parent_external_id, e.last_modified_at)\n                for e in entities\n            ],\n        )\n    conn.commit()\n```\n`executemany` pipelines the batch in one network round trip under psycopg3 (vs. one commit-per-row).\n\n**N+1-equivalent in `agent/graph.py::resolve()`.** If `resolve()` calls Claude once per conflict sequentially, a 5,000-conflict run makes 5,000 serial HTTP round trips. AGENTS.md already mandates prompt caching + the Batch API for `eval/` sweeps — apply the same batching discipline to the live path, bounded by concurrency:\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def resolve(conflicts: list[Conflict], rules: list[SurvivorshipRule]) -> list[Resolution]:\n    needs_model = [c for c in conflicts if select_rule(c, rules) is None]\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(10)  # cap concurrent Claude calls\n\n    async def propose_one(c: Conflict) -> Resolution:\n        async with sem:\n            return await propose(c)  # Sonnet call, ruleset block cache_control'd\n\n    model_results = await asyncio.gather(*(propose_one(c) for c in needs_model))\n    ...\n```\n\n**O(n²) blocking — already half-flagged in the stub's own docstring** (`blocking.py:3`: \"NEVER compare all pairs\"). Two things to hold the line on when you implement it:\n- Call `fold_name()` **once per entity** during `normalize()`, store it on `normalizedName`, and never re-fold inside the scoring loop (`blocking.py::score`). Folding N×top_k times instead of N times is redundant computation that compounds with dataset size.\n- Build the FAISS index once per side per run, not per candidate lookup — index construction is the expensive part; querying it is cheap.\n\n**Model/index loaded per-request instead of once at startup.** When `blocking.py` and `main.py` get implemented, load `sentence-transformers` model and any FAISS index construction at module import / FastAPI startup (`@app.on_event(\"startup\")` or a module-level singleton), not inside `execute_run()`. Re-loading a transformer model per HTTP request is the Python-service equivalent of a memory leak — it never gets GC'd fast enough between concurrent runs and multiplies RSS.\n\n**Future conflict-queue page (`/conflicts`) — join, don't loop.** The schema has `conflicts.matchId → matches.entityAId/entityBId → entities`. The queue UI needs entity names/values for both sides of every conflict in the row. Fetch with one Drizzle join, not N queries per row:\n```ts\nconst rows = await db\n  .select({ conflict: conflicts, match: matches, entityA: entitiesA, entityB: entitiesB })\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .innerJoin(matches, eq(conflicts.matchId, matches.id))\n  .innerJoin(entitiesA, eq(matches.entityAId, entitiesA.id))\n  .innerJoin(entitiesB, eq(matches.entityBId, entitiesB.id))\n  .where(eq(conflicts.runId, runId));\n```\n\n**Conflict queue re-renders (j/k keyboard nav, per AGENTS.md).** Once the diff-row list exists with hundreds of rows and keyboard-driven selection state, wrap `ConflictDiffRow` in `React.memo` so moving the selection cursor doesn't re-render every row — only the previously-selected and newly-selected rows should update:\n```tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ conflict, selected }: Props) {\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n**Run-status polling.** AGENTS.md scopes TanStack Query to exactly this. Use `useQuery`'s built-in `refetchInterval`, not a hand-rolled `useEffect`/`setInterval` — the manual version is the classic leak if the cleanup function is ever forgotten:\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: [\"run-status\", runId],\n  queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n  refetchInterval: (query) => (query.state.data?.status === \"complete\" ? false : 2000),\n});\n```\n`refetchInterval` returning `false` on terminal status also avoids polling forever after the run finishes — worth building that stop-condition in from the start rather than bolting it on later.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line\nNothing to fix urgently right now beyond the two `sources.ts`/`db/index.ts` items above (both small, both included). The higher-value move is treating the code examples above as the spec for Phase 2/3 (`blocking.py`, `db.py`, `graph.py`, `/conflicts`) so these patterns are correct on first implementation instead of needing a perf pass later.\n",
